the dawn of VR is upon us and now that  the price specs and requirements for the  PlayStation VR the oculus rift and the  HTC vive have been revealed you might be  wondering which one is right for you the  first thing you should probably look at  is price Sony's headset is the cheapest  of the bunch which comes in at around  four hundred dollars on the other end of  the spectrum is the vive which is 800  and finally you can get the oculus for  $600  but the headset isn't the only thing  you'll need to buy obviously the  PlayStation VR requires a ps4 but you'll  also need to pick up the PlayStation  camera for 60 bucks and if you want the  full experience you'll probably want to  get two PlayStation Move controllers  which are 50 apiece if you already own a  Playstation 4 then all this will  probably cost you around five hundred  and sixty dollars not including taxes  sony announced that there will be a  bundle at launch that will come with the  playstation camera and one move  controller but as of now we don't have a  price but even with all that PlayStation  VR is still cheaper than the oculus rift  the oculus rift is retailing at $600 and  it comes with a sensor integrated  headphones an oculus remote and an Xbox  one controller but once again if you  want the full experience you're probably  gonna want to pick up the oculus touch  controllers which as of now don't have a  price and won't launch with the headset  then there's the HTC vive this one is  the most expensive of the three for $800  you'll get the headset two wireless  controllers and two sensors besides a  compatible PC which is a hefty price in  itself the HTC vive comes with  everything you need to experience VR  right out of the box if you're wondering  what constitutes the price difference  here is where you'll find your answer  the resolution for PlayStation VR is  1920 by 1080 across two lenses or 960 by  1080 per eye while the oculus rift and  vive have a resolution of 2160 by 1200  or 1080 by 1200 per eye all three of  these devices use OLED displays and the  vibe and rift both have 110 degree field  of view while the PlayStation VR has a  hundred degree field of view one area  where the PlayStation VR comes out on  top is its refresh rate the vivan rift  both have a refresh rate of 90 Hertz  psvr on the other hand will have a  maximum refresh rate of 120  Hertz however one big difference between  PlayStation VR and the other two  headsets is that PlayStation VR will not  have a tracking area this means that  unlike the vive and oculus playstation  vr will not track walking movements the  vive will allow a 15 by 15 feet tracking  area and the oculus will have a 5 by 11  tracking area finally before you take  the plunge into VR you're going to want  to make sure that your rig can handle  these devices if you're leaning more  towards PlayStation VR all you need is a  ps4 the headset will also require an  external processor but that comes  packaged with the device but if you're  leaning more towards the oculus rift or  the HTC vive things get a bit more  complicated  both headsets will require an nvidia gtx  970 or AMD r9 290 or greater graphics  card in an Intel i5 4590 processor or  better you will also need at least eight  gigabytes of RAM for the oculus and at  least four for the HTC vive both  headsets will require an HDMI output and  USB ports the oculus will require four  USB ports three of which need to be USB  3.0 and one which can be USB 2.0 the  vibe on the other hand requires one USB  2.0 port if you don't have a PC capable  of running either of these headsets  oculus website can point you in the  direction of some oculus ready pcs the  oculus rift comes out March 28th and the  HTC vive along shortly after on April  5th if you're holding out for  PlayStation VR then you'll have to wait  until October  what headset are you leaning towards let  us know in the comments below and for  more VR stay tuned to game spot
 

